17592186520431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520431 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520431 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520431 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520431 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1b7f4c0-b2be-59fc-8152-805b6f3979f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520437 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea6d0dca-1729-5bc6-9b6f-8503ef6c372f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation ${x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a147b6b-b927-5c0b-b768-701655ad01fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520454 #edb/ref 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})+(3x-15) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-5)+3(x-5) & = \\\\ (x-5)\\cdot {x}^{2}+(x-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x-5)({x}^{2}+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x-5)({x}^{2}+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $x=5$ is the only real solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve ${n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34ca4779-2bf7-5602-9812-9d6d9723327c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520459 #edb/ref 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36 & = \\\\ ({n}^{3}+4{n}^{2})-(9n+36) & = \\\\ {n}^{2}(n+4)-9(n+4) & = \\\\ (n+4)\\cdot {n}^{2}-(n+4)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (n+4)({n}^{2}-9) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(n+4)({n}^{2}-9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(n+4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+4 & =0 \\\\ n & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({n}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =9 \\\\ n & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $n=-4,\\,\\pm 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $3{t}^{3}-15{t}^{2}-9t+45=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7651d9-4306-5281-ab2c-60e5fbcc4dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520461 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $3$, we start by dividing our equation through by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15 & = \\\\ ({t}^{3}-5{t}^{2})-(3t-15) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-5)-3(t-5) & = \\\\ (t-5)\\cdot {t}^{2}-(t-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (t-5)({t}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(t-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({t}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $5$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb562da5-ef3b-592a-bfc0-4d26f88b1f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520493 #edb/ref 1074 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36 & = \\\\ (36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2})-(16d-36) & = \\\\ 9{d}^{2}(4d-9)-4(4d-9) & = \\\\ (4d-9)\\cdot 9{d}^{2}-(4d-9)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ (4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(4d-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4d-9 & =0 \\\\ 4d & =9 \\\\ d & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{d}^{2}-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{d}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 9{d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $d=\\frac{9}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $-2{r}^{3}+12{r}^{2}+3r-18=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbddec8f-0415-5a7f-a473-3ac96a89f653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520494 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18 & = \\\\ (2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2})+(-3r+18) & = \\\\ 2{r}^{2}(r-6)-3(r-6) & = \\\\ (2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{r}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(r-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=6.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $r=6,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $y$ if ${y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-2y-10=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e76e6bf7-d54d-5018-81b2-f60c46589b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520507 #edb/ref 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-2y-10 & = \\\\ ({y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(2y+10) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}(y+5)-2(y+5) & = \\\\ (y+5)\\cdot {y}^{2}-(y+5)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (y+5)({y}^{2}-2) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(y+5)({y}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5 & =0 \\\\ y & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({y}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-5,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $30{n}^{3}+15{n}^{2}-20n-10=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6abbc6d-e05c-5882-80a0-628ebefcab1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520512 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $5$, we start by dividing our equation through by $5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2 & = \\\\ (6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2})-(4n+2) & = \\\\ 3{n}^{2}(2n+1)-2(2n+1) & = \\\\ (2n+1)\\cdot 3{n}^{2}-(2n+1)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (2n+1)(3{n}^{2}-2) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2n+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+1 & =0 \\\\ n & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3{n}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{n}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{n}^{2} & =2 \\\\ n & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve ${b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f84ff77e-8bf5-5075-9613-766131045ace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520515 #edb/ref 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12 & = \\\\ ({b}^{3}-2{b}^{2})-(6b-12) & = \\\\ {b}^{2}(b-2)-6(b-2) & = \\\\ (b-2)\\cdot {b}^{2}-(b-2)\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ (b-2)({b}^{2}-6) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(b-2)({b}^{2}-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(b-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({b}^{2}-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {b}^{2} & =6 \\\\ b & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $b=2,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $20{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-32y-16=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "faa06da2-05be-56dd-9e44-883b8d4527f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520517 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8 & = \\\\ (10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(16y+8) & = \\\\ 5{y}^{2}(2y+1)-8(2y+1) & = \\\\ (2y+1)\\cdot 5{y}^{2}-(2y+1)\\cdot 8 & = \\\\ (2y+1)(5{y}^{2}-8) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2y+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(5{y}^{2}-8)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
